  "summary": "SparkGLM on two directly connected DGX Sparks (TP-2 pair) or four cabled as a daisy chain (TP-4), serving nvidia/GLM-5.3-Flash-FP4-Local as published with DFlash2 speculative decoding: four requests of up to 1M tokens. Options: --sparks 2|4 and --drafter v81|v80|dflash2. Additive only: no default model, no aliases.",
  "description": "This recipe installs SparkGLM 0.3.5 at the immutable product revision recorded in backends/atlas-sparkglm/pins-0.3.5.json, on two Sparks (--sparks 2, a TP-2 pair over the private fabric) or four (--sparks 4, TP-4 over a switchless daisy chain, rank 0-1-2-3 along the cables), with any of three drafters (--drafter v81 or v80, Enntity's from a private repository, or --drafter dflash2, incoai/GLM-5.3-Flash-DFlash2). Without --drafter, both sizes use dflash2. Setup remembers the options it installed with. LLooM owns orchestration, admission, worker-first startup, readiness, routing and telemetry. The Atlas engine image is built from Enntity/sparkglm by that repository's install/build.sh; LLooM commits no engine source. The engine (Atlas-Inf/atlas, on the Enntity/atlas spark branch) is AGPL-3.0-only and runs from its own container image. Setup acquires the pinned checkpoint and the selected drafter and pulls the pinned image from GHCR by its immutable manifest digest (image.reference; the mutable tag is never pulled); the serving path only ever starts the prepared image. The checkpoint is served as published, with no overlay. One image serves both sizes. 0.3.5 is 0.3.4's engine plus three changes for concurrent streams on a pair in Spark mode, which this recipe runs; each leaves every token as it was. A request with a response_format grammar joins the shared verify pass instead of taking a target pass of its own every step (Enntity/atlas#30). Each stream in a shared pass verifies as many drafts as its own drafter was sure of (Enntity/atlas#31). A prompt longer than one 8K-token chunk that arrives while another is prefilling waits its turn, where it used to run its first chunk ahead of the chunks the earlier prompt still had to run (ATLAS_PREFILL_QUEUE_FIRST_CHUNK, set by the image's serve.py). None of the three is set on four Sparks. The engine also carries a prefill pass that yields to decoding streams between layers (Enntity/atlas#32); this version leaves it off. On a pair in Spark mode with dflash2 it qualified on 2026-11-15 against 0.3.4: identical greedy output and prompt logprobs, 109/109 live checks, and one RigMark round level with 0.3.4 (prose 38.7 against 38.8 tok/s, cold 16K prefill 2,856 against 2,849). Four JSON-schema streams decoded 68.5 tok/s combined against 43.1, and the first of four cold 16K-token prompts sent one second apart got its first token in 5.9 s against 11.2 s with the wave's length level (46.2 s against 45.7 s). On 2026-11-16 it was installed through this recipe; on a pair its Strict output and its image and video output were identical to 0.3.3's; and on a four-Spark chain its greedy output and prompt logprobs were identical to 0.3.0's, with 107/109 live checks (0.3.0: 105/109) and speed level. The four-Spark run used GPU memory utilization 0.82 under a 106 GB budget where this recipe sets 0.88 and 114 GB, because one Spark of that chain hosts other services; Strict mode and images were not run on four Sparks. It installs the same checkpoint and drafters as version 5.",
